<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arrian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 86–c. 160</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Arrian of Nicomedia, Lucius Flavius Arrianus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arrian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> was a Greek historian, philosopher and Roman official of the second century CE. A pupil of Epictetus, whose teachings he recorded in the Discourses and the Enchiridion, he governed Cappadocia under Hadrian and repelled an invasion by the Alans around 135. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a> of Alexander is the most important surviving account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s campaigns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arrian.jpg" alt="Arrian" width="220" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WikiSysop · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrian7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
